1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:02,704
Last time, we saw that chlorine pulls hard

2
00:00:02,865 --> 00:00:05,469
on its outer electrons, and sodium holds its

3
00:00:05,569 --> 00:00:06,811
outer electron loosely.

4
00:00:07,432 --> 00:00:09,780
Chemists measure that grip in two different ways.

5
00:00:10,504 --> 00:00:12,508
One asks how much energy it takes to

6
00:00:12,549 --> 00:00:14,193
pull an electron right off an atom.

7
00:00:14,735 --> 00:00:17,125
The other asks how strongly an atom pulls

8
00:00:17,167 --> 00:00:19,336
on electrons it shares with another atom.

9
00:00:19,940 --> 00:00:23,315
These are first ionisation energy and electronegativity.

10
00:00:23,979 --> 00:00:25,866
Let's see how both follow the structure of

11
00:00:25,887 --> 00:00:26,267
the atom.

12
00:00:27,141 --> 00:00:29,929
First ionisation energy is the energy needed to

13
00:00:29,989 --> 00:00:32,978
remove the outermost electron from each atom in

14
00:00:32,998 --> 00:00:34,623
a mole of gaseous atoms.

15
00:00:35,204 --> 00:00:37,371
A mole is a fixed, very large number

16
00:00:37,413 --> 00:00:37,813
of atoms.

17
00:00:38,417 --> 00:00:41,011
Gaseous just means the atoms are separate, with

18
00:00:41,072 --> 00:00:41,896
nothing else attached.

19
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:45,251
For sodium, each atom loses one electron and

20
00:00:45,272 --> 00:00:46,658
becomes a sodium ion.

21
00:00:47,202 --> 00:00:49,748
That takes four hundred and ninety-six kilojoules for

22
00:00:49,769 --> 00:00:51,193
a mole of sodium atoms.

23
00:00:51,735 --> 00:00:54,025
It's called first, because it removes only the

24
00:00:54,146 --> 00:00:56,376
first, most loosely held electron.

25
00:00:57,001 --> 00:00:58,789
Now go across Period three.

26
00:00:59,491 --> 00:01:02,856
From sodium to argon, the first ionisation energy

27
00:01:03,017 --> 00:01:03,858
generally rises,

28
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:06,964
from four hundred and ninety-six, to fifteen hundred

29
00:01:06,984 --> 00:01:08,807
and twenty-one kilojoules per mole.

30
00:01:09,449 --> 00:01:10,876
That's the same cause as last time.

31
00:01:11,319 --> 00:01:13,924
The core charge climbs while the electrons stay

32
00:01:14,004 --> 00:01:14,886
in the same shell,

33
00:01:15,467 --> 00:01:17,711
so the atoms get smaller, and the outer

34
00:01:17,751 --> 00:01:19,554
electrons are held more tightly.

35
00:01:20,197 --> 00:01:21,963
It takes more energy to pull one off.

36
00:01:22,887 --> 00:01:25,573
Removing chlorine's outer electron takes about two and

37
00:01:25,594 --> 00:01:27,759
a half times the energy needed for sodium's.

38
00:01:28,390 --> 00:01:29,381
But look closely.

39
00:01:30,009 --> 00:01:31,884
The rise isn't perfectly smooth.

40
00:01:32,811 --> 00:01:34,797
Aluminium is lower than magnesium.

41
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:38,063
Aluminium's outer electron is the first one in

42
00:01:38,104 --> 00:01:40,588
a three p subshell, which is higher in

43
00:01:40,649 --> 00:01:43,713
energy than magnesium's three s, so it's easier

44
00:01:43,754 --> 00:01:44,194
to remove.

45
00:01:45,018 --> 00:01:47,393
And sulfur is slightly lower than phosphorus.

46
00:01:47,977 --> 00:01:50,804
Sulfur's fourth three p electron has to share

47
00:01:50,845 --> 00:01:52,388
an orbital with another electron.

48
00:01:52,971 --> 00:01:54,740
The two repel each other, so one of

49
00:01:54,761 --> 00:01:56,048
them is easier to remove.

50
00:01:56,893 --> 00:02:00,981
Exam answers say first ionisation energy generally increases

51
00:02:01,002 --> 00:02:01,903
across a period.

52
00:02:02,612 --> 00:02:03,221
Quick prediction.

53
00:02:03,849 --> 00:02:05,658
Lithium is at the top of Group one,

54
00:02:06,081 --> 00:02:07,407
and caesium is near the bottom.

55
00:02:08,012 --> 00:02:09,980
Caesium has fifty-two more protons.

56
00:02:10,684 --> 00:02:12,812
Which one needs more energy to lose its

57
00:02:12,953 --> 00:02:13,615
outer electron?

58
00:02:14,305 --> 00:02:14,670
Have a think.

59
00:02:18,424 --> 00:02:18,768
Lithium.

60
00:02:19,781 --> 00:02:22,010
Going down Group one, each element has an

61
00:02:22,170 --> 00:02:23,355
extra occupied shell,

62
00:02:23,857 --> 00:02:25,704
so the outer electron is further from the

63
00:02:25,744 --> 00:02:26,146
nucleus.

64
00:02:26,889 --> 00:02:29,461
The core charge stays at plus one, so

65
00:02:29,501 --> 00:02:30,827
the extra protons don't help.

66
00:02:31,591 --> 00:02:34,459
Further away means a weaker attraction, so less

67
00:02:34,519 --> 00:02:35,722
energy removes the electron.

68
00:02:36,505 --> 00:02:39,090
First ionisation energy falls from five hundred and

69
00:02:39,131 --> 00:02:41,977
twenty for lithium, to three hundred and seventy-six

70
00:02:42,179 --> 00:02:42,819
for caesium.

71
00:02:43,463 --> 00:02:44,476
Now the second measure.

72
00:02:45,061 --> 00:02:47,388
In a covalent bond, two atoms share a

73
00:02:47,549 --> 00:02:48,431
pair of electrons.

74
00:02:49,173 --> 00:02:51,820
Electronegativity is how strongly an atom in a

75
00:02:51,860 --> 00:02:53,584
bond attracts that shared pair.

76
00:02:54,406 --> 00:02:55,912
Think of a tug of war, with the

77
00:02:55,952 --> 00:02:57,016
shared pair as the rope.

78
00:02:57,658 --> 00:02:59,864
The atom with the stronger pull draws the

79
00:02:59,904 --> 00:03:00,926
pair toward itself.

80
00:03:02,011 --> 00:03:03,568
Electronegativity has no units.

81
00:03:03,993 --> 00:03:06,241
It's a scale, called the Pauling scale, and

82
00:03:06,322 --> 00:03:08,852
fluorine is the strongest, at four point zero.

83
00:03:09,944 --> 00:03:12,279
Electronegativity follows the same atomic structure.

84
00:03:12,922 --> 00:03:15,683
Across Period three, the core charge rises and

85
00:03:15,703 --> 00:03:17,684
the atoms shrink, so the pull on a

86
00:03:17,744 --> 00:03:20,626
shared pair gets stronger, from zero point nine

87
00:03:20,646 --> 00:03:23,047
for sodium to three point two for chlorine.

88
00:03:24,207 --> 00:03:27,089
Down Group seventeen, each extra shell puts the

89
00:03:27,129 --> 00:03:29,450
shared pair further from the nucleus, so the

90
00:03:29,470 --> 00:03:32,052
pull weakens, from four point zero for fluorine

91
00:03:32,332 --> 00:03:33,893
to two point seven for iodine.

92
00:03:34,735 --> 00:03:37,043
So the most electronegative elements sit at the

93
00:03:37,125 --> 00:03:39,192
top right of the table, and the least

94
00:03:39,433 --> 00:03:40,155
at the bottom left.

95
00:03:40,975 --> 00:03:43,158
So both measures rise across a period and

96
00:03:43,259 --> 00:03:45,102
fall down a group, but they're not the

97
00:03:45,143 --> 00:03:45,583
same thing.

98
00:03:46,446 --> 00:03:49,618
Ionisation energy is an energy, for one separate

99
00:03:49,699 --> 00:03:50,964
atom losing an electron.

100
00:03:51,727 --> 00:03:54,131
Electronegativity is a number on a scale, for

101
00:03:54,152 --> 00:03:55,734
an atom in a bond pulling on a

102
00:03:55,795 --> 00:03:56,435
shared pair.

103
00:03:57,480 --> 00:03:58,875
The noble gases show the difference.

104
00:03:59,521 --> 00:04:02,093
Argon has the highest first ionisation energy in

105
00:04:02,154 --> 00:04:02,756
Period three.

106
00:04:03,300 --> 00:04:06,044
But noble gases almost never form bonds, so

107
00:04:06,126 --> 00:04:08,750
they usually have no electronegativity value at all.

108
00:04:09,493 --> 00:04:10,419
That doesn't mean zero.

109
00:04:10,803 --> 00:04:12,098
It means there's nothing to measure.

110
00:04:12,808 --> 00:04:14,271
So here's a picture to keep.

111
00:04:14,914 --> 00:04:16,238
Top right holds tight,

112
00:04:16,801 --> 00:04:18,065
bottom left lets go.

113
00:04:20,721 --> 00:04:21,088
Your turn.

114
00:04:22,017 --> 00:04:25,243
The first ionisation energy of sodium is four

115
00:04:25,304 --> 00:04:27,648
hundred and ninety-six kilojoules per mole,

116
00:04:28,150 --> 00:04:30,515
and for potassium it's four hundred and nineteen.

117
00:04:31,318 --> 00:04:34,128
Explain this difference in terms of atomic structure.

118
00:04:34,914 --> 00:04:35,727
Pause here to answer.

119
00:04:46,334 --> 00:04:49,541
Potassium has one more occupied shell, so its

120
00:04:49,662 --> 00:04:51,746
outer electron is further from the nucleus.

121
00:04:52,489 --> 00:04:54,614
The core charge is plus one for both,

122
00:04:54,935 --> 00:04:57,361
so the extra protons are balanced by extra

123
00:04:57,482 --> 00:04:58,263
inner electrons.

124
00:04:58,926 --> 00:05:01,254
A weaker attraction means less energy is needed

125
00:05:01,295 --> 00:05:01,816
to remove it.

126
00:05:02,678 --> 00:05:05,499
Saying potassium has more protons, so it holds

127
00:05:05,620 --> 00:05:06,080
tighter,

128
00:05:06,601 --> 00:05:07,701
is the classic slip.

129
00:05:08,345 --> 00:05:09,751
Distance and shielding win.

130
00:05:11,461 --> 00:05:11,869
Last one.

131
00:05:12,578 --> 00:05:15,698
Look across Period three, from sodium to argon.

132
00:05:16,281 --> 00:05:19,329
Which element has the highest first ionisation energy,

133
00:05:19,831 --> 00:05:21,996
and which has the highest electronegativity?

134
00:05:22,660 --> 00:05:24,437
Explain why the answers are different.

135
00:05:25,044 --> 00:05:25,769
Pause here to answer.

136
00:05:36,562 --> 00:05:39,777
Argon has the highest first ionisation energy, at

137
00:05:39,858 --> 00:05:41,064
fifteen hundred and twenty-one.

138
00:05:41,747 --> 00:05:45,043
Chlorine has the highest electronegativity, at three point

139
00:05:45,083 --> 00:05:45,284
two.

140
00:05:46,188 --> 00:05:49,431
They differ because electronegativity describes an atom in

141
00:05:49,471 --> 00:05:52,515
a bond, and argon almost never forms bonds,

142
00:05:52,815 --> 00:05:53,916
so it has no value.

143
00:05:54,758 --> 00:05:57,387
First ionisation energy can be measured for any

144
00:05:57,507 --> 00:05:58,209
separate atom.

145
00:05:58,973 --> 00:06:00,960
Answering argon for both is the trap.

146
00:06:03,495 --> 00:06:04,503
Here's where this goes next.

147
00:06:05,250 --> 00:06:07,743
Group one metals react by losing their one

148
00:06:07,966 --> 00:06:08,689
outer electron.

149
00:06:09,212 --> 00:06:10,952
And we've just seen that losing it gets

150
00:06:11,133 --> 00:06:13,474
easier as you go down the group, from

151
00:06:13,535 --> 00:06:15,816
lithium, to sodium, to potassium.

152
00:06:16,598 --> 00:06:18,865
So what happens when each of these metals

153
00:06:18,986 --> 00:06:19,687
meets water?

154
00:06:20,411 --> 00:06:22,476
Next time, we'll find out why Group one

155
00:06:22,517 --> 00:06:24,502
metals react differently with water.

156
00:06:27,195 --> 00:06:28,017
Here are the key notes.

157
00:06:28,541 --> 00:06:30,088
Pause the video if you need more time

158
00:06:30,129 --> 00:06:30,550
to copy them.
